#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Set up an Orca project with a coordinator: on native Orca (the default), or run by Cotal
agents (--orchestrator orca-cotal; see orchestrators/orca-cotal/docs/orca.md).

  project-setup <git-url|dir> [<host>] [--orchestrator native-orca|orca-cotal] [--name N] [--dest DIR] [--coordinator HARNESS/MODEL]
                [--permission-mode auto|bypassPermissions|default]
                [--port N] [--tiers FILE] [--no-start]
                [--private GLOB]... [--adapter HARNESS/MODEL] [--adapt-reviewer HARNESS/MODEL]
                [--gateway-url URL] [--gateway-key-env NAME] [--trust-gateway]
                [--readapt | --skip-adapt | --refresh | --show] [--write-orca-yaml] [--with <dir|git-url>]...
                [--no-desktop-lanes] [--pair-by-hand] [--no-machine-setup] [--orca-port N]
                [--orca-from DIR | --orca-bundle TAR] [--orca-version V]
                [--tunnel-auth-sock PATH] [--tunnel-ssh PATH] [--tunnel-no-enable] [--tunnel-remote-port N]
  project-setup --remove-host <host>
  project-setup --check <dir> | <git-url|dir> <host>
  project-setup <dir> --suggest-tiers <provider> [--gateway-url URL] [--gateway-key-env NAME]

Native Orca (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-19-native-orca-setup-design.md, section 11): the same onboarding (an
adapting agent and a reviewer, committed only on the owner's yes; the role files go to .lane-control/additions/ and
AGENTS.lane-control.md gets the lane-control and native-orca sections, AGENTS.md one pointer line to it), then the
personas of the coordinator, reviewer, design reviewer and debugger rendered into .lane-control/<role>.md with the
project's additions at the end (kept out of git by .git/info/exclude), the persona hook of the coordinator's harness
(core/harnesses/), the design kit (the component-gallery skill, the frontend brief, design-shots and the design
skills from core/design-skills.tsv, for Claude and OpenCode), and the coordinator started in its own Orca terminal
titled "<project> coordinator", which makes the session's own Orca Run before the agent starts (its id goes in the
settings store). Whether a coordinator already runs is decided by its processes, never by a terminal's title or Orca's
agent mark: proven, it is left alone; recorded with no agent, it is said as `recorded but dead` and started again; and
when that cannot be told (say its agent was restored by Orca into a sandbox whose environment cannot be read), it is
said as `cannot tell` and nothing is started, so there is never a second one. The
main checkout's CLAUDE.local.md gets the coordinator's persona anchor (core/anchor.py): a session started there by hand
is told to read the persona; the rest of that file is kept, and it is never committed. The design lead is retired (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-21-design-kit-design.md): what is left of
one is moved or named, never deleted silently; --design-lead is refused and --no-design-lead does nothing. Claude and
OpenCode (v2, opencode2) both take every role; nothing is ever typed into a terminal. A project keeps the
orchestrator it was set up with (the store, or a .cotal/ folder) unless --orchestrator names another.

With a host (`<host>` after the repo, or --host; the repo is then a URL or a path on the host), native Orca runs it
there and drives it from here (orchestrators/native-orca/remote.md): Orca's server on the host, paired here
(orca-host-install, when it does not answer yet); lane-control copied to the host and the steps above run there by
its own copy; then, unless --no-desktop-lanes, the desktop's link for the host (in a terminal only): by default made
here with `orca pair --address 127.0.0.1:<box port> --json` after setup says what it grants, that this desktop's Orca
will listen on every interface and how to revoke it, and the owner says yes; with --pair-by-hand, or on a no, the share
link the owner makes in Orca, asked for on a hidden prompt (it keeps the desktop on loopback). Either goes to the host
on ssh's stdin and is saved there as an Orca environment, with a reverse tunnel kept open here (core/scripts/
lane-tunnel, a systemd user service), so the host's coordinator can run lanes here; then machine-setup there, unless
--no-machine-setup. The link is never in arguments, logs, files or the store: only the environment's name is kept.
--check <repo> <host> lists every prerequisite of that run, and the box's persona anchors, and changes nothing.
--check <dir>, with no host, says whether this machine's project has its coordinator persona and anchor as setup writes
them (exit 1 if not), and changes nothing. --remove-host undoes the desktop lanes and says where to revoke the link. A
project
the host runs on Orca + Cotal keeps that path, as does --orchestrator orca-cotal.

Runs on the execution host as a normal user (never root) and needs a reachable Orca runtime (`orca status`), `cotal`
with the @cotal-ai/orca extension, and the harnesses it will launch. Every step is skipped when already done; a
re-run reports `exists` for each.

Before the Orca steps, a headless adapting agent writes the project's files into .lane-control/ and a reviewer on
another model checks them (--adapter, --adapt-reviewer: `claude -p` for claude/<model>, `opencode2 run` for
opencode/<provider>/<model>; only the harness a role uses must be on PATH). An OpenCode one reaches its gateway only by
--gateway-url (written out plainly, not a known vendor's API) and --gateway-key-env NAME (the key's variable, handed to
the run as LANE_GATEWAY_KEY), both kept per project (never the key; a new URL does not take the old key variable along
unasked), under the run's own provider id lane-gateway, with OpenCode's config built from nothing; it works in a
filtered scratch copy of the repo, or with --trust-gateway (a plain warning and one yes; for a model on a gateway the
owner runs) in the repo itself, every file readable, its writes checked before and after (a refused one stops setup
before any commit and any further git command, and the next run asks about it before its first git command). No git
command this process runs runs a program the repo names (GitSafePopen: hooks, core.fsmonitor, filters, drivers off;
ssh, credential and gpg programs the owner's global ones). --skip-adapt runs no
onboarding agent (a machine with no usable model yet: adapt later with --readapt). They are committed, by explicit
path, only on the owner's yes in a terminal; on no, or without a terminal, they stay uncommitted, no Orca step runs and it exits 3.
The repo's visibility is read with gh (unknown when it cannot be); questions still open after the review are shown and
may be answered before the file list (one more adapter run, not reviewed). After that commit it asks once more, in a
terminal: to commit a drafted orca.yaml (from the lockfiles; Orca reads its setup script from the worktree) and to push
the default branch, since Orca makes lane worktrees from origin/<branch>. A no, no terminal or a failed push commits and
pushes nothing more and the agents still start. Nothing else is ever committed or pushed.

--with attaches a second repo to the project (e.g. an app and its plugins repo): one space, one manager, one
coordinator, rooted at the first repo; the attached one gets lanes from that coordinator and nothing else of its own. It
gets the per-repo work the first one gets (Orca registration, the stale-checkout refusal, the private-file deny, the
adapted files and generated sections with AGENTS.md's pointer line, orca.yaml, the same commit rules) but no space,
manager or coordinator. --private globs apply to every repo of the project, each relative to its own root. The attached repos are kept in the project's settings store and covered by later runs, --refresh and --show.
A repo that is its own project, or attached to another, is refused. Lanes branch from each repo's own default branch.

--refresh is for a project set up earlier, after lane-control's personas or skill changed: it re-renders the personas
in .cotal/agents/ (keeping each one's harness, model and permission mode), reinstalls the skill, leaves the project's
tier table alone and says which running agents keep the old text until restarted. No adapting or reviewing
agent, no broker, no Orca worktree, no commit.

The tier table is decided once per project: a full --tiers, else the defaults (orchestrators/orca-cotal/templates/lanes.tsv). A re-run keeps the
project's table unless --tiers gives another. Nothing is refused by model or harness (owner 2026-11-24): the project's
table is the only place that says which models it uses.

Before a role's pin or a tier table is written with a model it did not have, the gateway that model goes through is
asked what it serves (one GET <base>/v1/models; core/modelenv.py): ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL for Claude (from model-env's
source, your Claude settings or this shell, never from a repo), the provider's baseURL in OpenCode's config for
OpenCode. A model it does not list is not written. One it could not confirm (no answer, a refused key, an alias like
`opus` with no ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_OPUS_MODEL to say what is sent, only a wildcard) is written only on your yes in a
terminal; without a terminal it is not written. --no-model-check writes without the check. With no gateway nothing is
asked or said.

--suggest-tiers <provider> suggests that table from an OpenAI-compatible gateway (a self-hosted LiteLLM, say): GET
<base>/v1/models, and <base>/model/info where it answers (LiteLLM: the model behind each alias). Each row is
opencode/<provider>/<alias>, <provider> being the gateway's provider id in OpenCode's config. The base URL is
--gateway-url (the onboarding agents' flag; --gateway is its old name), else the project's stored gateway, else that
provider's options.baseURL in OpenCode's own config; the key only an environment variable, the one that goes with that
URL: --gateway-key-env, else the stored gateway's, else the {env:NAME} its options.apiKey names. OpenCode's auth files are never read; the key is never printed, logged,
stored or put in an argv (core/tiersuggest.py). It shows the suggestion with each alias's model and asks, in a terminal,
whether to write it as the project's lanes.tsv; nothing else runs, and on no (or no terminal) nothing is written and
it exits 3: edit the table shown and pass it with --tiers.

Per-project settings live outside the repo and outside .cotal/ (which Cotal owns), in
~/.config/lane-control/projects/<folder name>-<short hash of its path>/: settings.json (models per role, permission mode,
--private globs, broker port) and lanes.tsv (the tier table). Flags update
the store and a run without them reuses it; --show prints it. A project set up before the store existed is moved there
once, automatically, and the run says what moved. Only the rendered personas stay in .cotal/agents/. No secret goes in.
"""
